claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> On Tue, 13 Oct 2026 22:20:42 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
>> > So nothing is lost, but nothing retries for you either. If you want
>> > mail written offline to go out by itself, the queue has to live in
>> > your client, not in lab-mail. msmtp ships msmtpq, a wrapper that
>> > queues locally when the network is down and sends the queue later.
>> > I have not checked how it treats a 5xx. It must not queue a permanent
>> > rejection, or it brings back the problem the relay solves.
>>
>> What's a better approach here? Error early and save the draft, and
>> manually re-send? Or queue it up and have it auto-deliver later?
>
> Error early, keep the message, and re-send by hand. Three reasons:
>
> - You asked that the sender always learn when a send fails and why.
>   That is what ruled out a queuing MTA in lab-mail. A client queue
>   answers "sent" before Fastmail has seen the message, so it moves
>   the same problem one hop closer to you instead of removing it.
> - An agent is worse off with a queue than you are. process-email
>   checks results from outside, and a non-zero msmtp exit is a fact
>   it can act on: stop, and send again on the next pass. A message
>   sitting in msmtpq's queue is a state no pass looks at.
> - The cost is small. Fastmail outages are rare and short, and while
>   you are on beastie, reaching lab-mail is never the problem. A
>   failed send leaves the compose buffer open; C-c C-p files it under
>   your drafts search, and you send it from there.

This is good reasoning and should be included in the spec. As a rule,
specs should have alternatives considered as well. Essentially when
reading the spec we should be able to answer 1) what are we doing and
why and 2) what are we not doing and why?

